seg-553cef940bfc = <a1><c1/></a1> holds what every tool shares: writing the three files through the Python binding (their SHA-256 must be the pinned ones, and the command line's bytes when <c2/> names its output), reading back what was written, and the difference report.

# text
seg-1e04db4a3409 = The difference report

# text
seg-200a9d596b9c = For every file a tool reads, value by value:

# text
seg-b1958aa7d766 = <b>Values with the written bits.</b> For a tool that holds the file's units (km, km/s).

# text
seg-39e66aa247fa = <b>Values equal to the tool's own conversion.</b> A tool that holds other units must hold exactly its own conversion of the written double. Orekit holds metres, so each value must be the written kilometre value times { "1000.0" }.

# text
seg-fd745ac010c5 = <b>Epochs equal.</b> Each epoch the tool holds equals the written TDB label.

# text
seg-453c7d9d656f = <b>Worst error in half-units of the last digit written,</b> and in ulps from the correctly rounded value. Above { "1" } half-unit, the report names the tool as one that rounds further than the digits written.

# text
seg-8b76c756a1e4 = A file is <b>carried</b> when every value is the written double, or the tool's own conversion of it, and every epoch is the written one.

# text
seg-b9dbaa84b945 = Some tools evaluate a kernel and do not hand back its stored records (ANISE's Python API). For those, the states at the node epochs are compared with the written states, beside SPICE's own evaluation of Kshana's SPK of the same states. The file is carried when every epoch is the written one and no state is further from the written one than SPICE's evaluation is. The report marks these rows "evaluated, not read". For a tool whose only readout is a fixed-precision text report (GMAT, { "16" } significant digits), SPICE's evaluation is printed at the same precision before the comparison, so both pass through the same readout (<a1>NAMING.md</a1>, the 8-9 November amendment).

